== Chapter IX

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[Particular directions for the case above proposed — #smallcaps[First]. Consider the dangerous symptoms of any lust — 1. Long standing — 2. Peace obtained under it; the several ways this is done — 3. Frequent success in its seductions — 4. The soul’s fighting against it only with arguments taken from the outcome — 5. Judicial hardness accompanying it — 6. Its withstanding particular dealings from God — The state of persons in whom these things are found.]

=== III. Particular Directions

#smallcaps[With] these #emph[general rules] laid down, the next thing to propose is #emph[particular directions] to guide the soul when it feels a disquieting lust or disorder, and this is the main thing I aim at. Some of these directions are preliminary and preparatory; in others the work itself is contained. The #emph[first kind] are these:

==== First Direction: Consider the Dangerous Symptoms of the Lust

#smallcaps[First]. Consider what dangerous #emph[symptoms] come with your lust or accompany it, whether it has any deadly mark on it or not. If it has, #emph[extraordinary] remedies must be used; an ordinary course of mortification will not do it.

You#super[+] will say, “What are these dangerous marks and #emph[symptoms] you mean, the desperate #emph[signs] that come with an indwelling lust?” I will name some of them:

1\. #emph[Long standing.] If it has lain corrupting in your heart for a long time, if you have allowed it to remain in power and prevail for some long season without vigorously trying to kill it and to heal the wounds you have received from it, your disorder is dangerous. Have you for some long season allowed worldliness, ambition, or greed for study to eat up other duties, the duties in which you ought to hold constant communion with God? Or have you allowed uncleanness to defile your heart with vain, foolish, and wicked imaginations for many days? Your lust has a dangerous symptom. So it was with David: “My wounds are foul and festering because of my sinful folly” (Ps 38:5). When a lust has lain long in the heart, corrupting, festering, eating away like a cancer, it brings the soul into a woeful condition. In such a case an ordinary course of humbling oneself will not do the work. Whatever the lust is, by this means it will work its way more or less into all the faculties of the soul and accustom the affections to its company and society. It grows familiar to the mind and conscience, so that they do not start at it as at a strange thing, but are bold with it as with something they are used to. Yes, it gains such an advantage by this means that it often exerts itself and acts without being noticed at all, as seems to have been the case with Joseph when he swore by the life of Pharaoh. Unless some extraordinary course is taken, such a person has no ground in the world to expect that his latter end will be peace.

For, first, how will he be able to tell the difference between the long stay of an #emph[unmortified lust] and the #emph[dominion of sin], which cannot befall a regenerate person? Secondly, how can he promise himself that it will ever be otherwise with him, or that his lust will stop raging and seducing? He sees it fixed and staying, as it has for many days, and it has gone through a #emph[variety] of conditions with him. Perhaps it has been tried with #emph[mercies] and #emph[afflictions], and those perhaps so remarkable that the soul could not help taking special notice of them. Perhaps it has weathered many a storm and come through a great variety of gifts in the ministry of the word. Will it be an easy thing, then, to evict a lodger who pleads a right of long possession? Old neglected wounds are often fatal, and always dangerous. Indwelling disorders grow rusty and stubborn by continuing in ease and quiet. Lust is a lodger that will not easily be thrown out if it can plead time and some right of possession. As it never dies of itself, so if it is not killed daily it will always gather strength.

2\. Another dangerous symptom of a deadly disorder in the heart is this: the heart secretly pleads to #emph[excuse] itself and keep up its peace while a lust remains, without a vigorous gospel effort to mortify it. There are several ways this may be done. I will name some of them:

(1.) When a man has #emph[thoughts] about sin, perplexing thoughts, and instead of applying himself to destroy it, he searches his heart to see what evidence he can find that his condition is good in spite of that sin and lust, so that it may go well with him.

For a man to gather up his experiences of God, to call them to mind, collect them, consider, test, and make use of them, is an excellent thing. It is a duty practiced by all the saints and commended in the Old Testament and the New. This was David’s work when he mused in his heart and called to remembrance the former loving kindness of the Lord.#footnote[Ps 77:6–9.] This is the duty Paul sets us to practice (2 Cor 13:5). And as it is excellent in itself, so beauty is added to it by a fitting season: a time of trial or temptation, or of the heart’s disquiet about sin. Such a time is a setting of silver to set off this apple of gold, as Solomon speaks. But to do it for this purpose, to quiet a conscience that cries and calls for something else, is a desperate device of a heart in love with sin. Conscience deals with a man; God rebukes him for the sinful disorder of his heart. If, instead of applying himself to get that sin #emph[pardoned] in the blood of Christ and #emph[mortified] by his Spirit, he relieves himself with #emph[such other evidences] as he has, or thinks he has, and so frees himself from under the yoke God was putting on his neck, his condition is very dangerous and his wound hardly curable. So the Jews, galled by their own consciences and by the convicting preaching of our Savior, held themselves up with this: that they were “Abraham’s children,” and accepted with God on that account. And so they excused themselves in all abominable wickedness, to their utter ruin.

This is, in some degree, a man blessing himself and saying that on one account or another he will have peace, “although he adds drunkenness to thirst.” Love of sin, and a low value on peace and on every taste of love from God, are wrapped up in such a frame. Such a man plainly shows that if he can only keep up a hope of escaping “the coming wrath,” he is well content to be unfruitful in the world, at any distance from God short of final separation. What can be expected from such a heart?

(2.) This deceit is also carried on by applying #emph[grace and mercy] to a sin that is not mortified, or that a man is not #emph[sincerely] trying to mortify. This is a sign of a heart greatly entangled with the love of sin. When a man has secret thoughts in his heart not unlike Naaman’s about his worship in the house of Rimmon,#footnote[2 Kgs 5:18.] “In all other things I will walk with God, but in this one thing, may God be merciful to me,” his condition is sad. It is true that a resolution of this kind, to indulge oneself in any sin on account of mercy, seems to be, and in any settled course certainly is, altogether inconsistent with Christian sincerity. It is the badge of a hypocrite, and is to “turn the grace of our God into a license for immorality.”#footnote[Jude 4.] Yet I do not doubt that through Satan’s craft and their own remaining unbelief, the children of God themselves may sometimes be ensnared by this deceit of sin. Otherwise Paul would never have warned them against it as he does (Rom 6:1, 2). Indeed, nothing is more natural than for fleshly reasonings to grow loud and strong on this account. The flesh would gladly be indulged on account of grace. It stands ready to catch at every word spoken of mercy and to twist it to its own corrupt aims and purposes. To apply mercy, then, to a sin that is not vigorously mortified is to make the gospel serve the purpose of the flesh.

A deceitful heart will sometimes use these and many other ways and tricks to excuse itself in its abominations. Now suppose a man and his sin are in this condition: a secret liking for the sin prevails in his heart. His will is not wholly set on it, yet he has a faint wish toward it; he would practice it were it not for this or that consideration. And on this footing he relieves himself in other ways than by the mortification and pardon of it in the blood of Christ. That man’s “wounds are foul and festering,” and without speedy deliverance he will be at the door of death.

3\. Another dangerous symptom is #emph[frequent success] in sin’s seduction, in gaining the will’s prevailing consent to it. This is what I mean: when the sin we are speaking of gets the consent of the will with some #emph[delight], then it has success, even if it is not actually carried out outwardly. For outward reasons a man may not be able to go along with sin to what James calls its being “full-grown,”#footnote[Jas 1:14, 15.] as far as the outward acts of sin go, while the will to sin may actually have been gained. Then, I say, it has success. Now, if any lust can prevail this far in any man’s soul, his condition may possibly be very bad, and he himself unregenerate. At any rate it cannot possibly be very good; it is dangerous. And it comes to the same thing whether this is done by the choice of the will or by inadvertence, for that inadvertence is itself in a manner chosen. When we are inadvertent and negligent where we are bound to be watchful and careful, that inadvertence does not make what we do as a result any less voluntary. Men do not choose and resolve to be negligent and inadvertent. Yet if they choose the things that will make them so, they choose inadvertence itself, as a thing may be chosen in its cause.

And let no man think that the evil of his heart is in any way lessened because, for the most part, he seems to be taken by surprise into the consent he seems to give to sin. It is his negligence in the duty of watching over his heart that betrays him into that surprise.

4\. When a man fights against his sin only with #emph[arguments from the outcome], or from the punishment due to it, it is a sign that sin has taken great possession of the will, and that in the heart there is an overflowing of wickedness. The man who opposes nothing to the seduction of sin and lust in his heart but fear of shame among men or of hell from God is well enough resolved to do the sin if no punishment came with it. And how that differs from living in the practice of sin, I do not know. Those who are Christ’s, and are moved in their obedience by gospel principles, have the death of Christ, the love of God, the detestable nature of sin, the preciousness of communion with God, and a deep-rooted hatred of sin #emph[as sin], to oppose to every seduction of sin, to all the workings, strivings, and fightings of lust in their hearts. So it was with Joseph. “How could I do such a great evil,” he says, “and sin against God?”—my good and gracious God.#footnote[Gen 39:9.] And Paul: “Christ’s love compels us”;#footnote[2 Cor 5:14.] and, “Since we have these promises, let us cleanse ourselves from everything that defiles body and spirit” (2 Cor 7:1). But suppose a man is so under the power of his lust that he has nothing but the law to oppose it with. He cannot fight against it with gospel weapons, but deals with it entirely with hell and judgment, which are the proper weapons of the law. Then it is plain that sin has taken possession of his will and affections, prevailing over them and conquering them to a very great degree.

As far as this particular sin goes, such a person has thrown off the guidance of #emph[renewing] grace, and is kept from ruin only by #emph[restraining] grace. To that extent he has fallen from grace and returned under the power of the law. And can anyone think it is not a great provocation to Christ that men should throw off his easy and gentle yoke and rule, and put themselves under the iron yoke of the law, merely to indulge their lusts?

Test yourself by this too. When sin brings you to the point where you must either serve it and rush into folly at its command, like a horse charging into battle, or else take a stand against it to suppress it, what do you say to your soul? What do you argue with yourself? Is this all: “Hell will be the end of this course; vengeance will meet me and find me out”? It is time for you to look about you; evil lies at the door. Paul’s main argument to prove that sin will not have dominion over believers is that they are “not under law, but under grace” (Rom 6:14). If your fighting against sin is all on legal grounds, from legal principles and motives, what assurance can you reach that sin will not have dominion over you, which would be your ruin?

Yes, and know that this last line of defense will not hold out long. If your lust has driven you out of the stronger forts of the gospel, it will soon prevail against this one too. Do not suppose that such considerations will deliver you, when you have willingly given up to your enemy the helps and means of preservation that have a thousand times their strength. Rest assured of this: unless you quickly recover yourself from this condition, the thing you fear will come upon you. What #emph[gospel principles] do not do, #emph[legal motives] cannot do.

5\. Another dangerous symptom is when it is likely that there is, or may be, something of judicial hardening, or at least of chastening punishment, in your lust as it disquiets you. I have no doubt at all that God sometimes leaves even some of his own under the perplexing power of some lust or sin, at least, to correct them for former sins, negligence, and folly. That is why the church complained, “Why, O #smallcaps[Lord], do You make us stray from Your ways and harden our hearts from fearing You?” (Isa 63:17). No man questions that this is his way of dealing with unregenerate men. But how can a man know whether God’s chastening hand has any part in his being left to the disquiet of his disorder? #emph[Answer.] Examine your heart and your ways. What was the state and condition of your soul before you fell into the entanglements of the sin you now complain of so much? Had you been negligent in duties? Had you been living self-indulgently? Is the guilt of some great sin lying on you, unrepented of? A #emph[new sin may be allowed], as well as a #emph[new affliction sent], to bring an old sin to remembrance.

Have you received some outstanding mercy, protection, or deliverance that you did not make good use of, or were not thankful for? Or have you been exercised with some affliction without laboring for the end it was appointed for? Or have you failed to use the opportunities of glorifying God in your generation that he had graciously given you in his good providence? Or have you conformed yourself to the world and the men of it, through the abundance of temptations in the days you live in? If you find that this has been your state, wake up and call on God. You are fast asleep in a storm of anger all around you.

6\. When your lust has already #emph[withstood] particular dealings from God against it. This condition is described in Isa 57:17: “I was enraged by his sinful greed, so I struck him and hid My face in anger; yet he kept turning back to the desires of his heart.” God had dealt with them about their prevailing lust, and in several ways, by affliction and by withdrawing from them, but they held out against it all. This is a sad condition. Nothing but #emph[sheer sovereign grace] (as God expresses it in the next verse) can relieve a man in it, and no man ought to promise himself that grace, or presume upon it. In his providential dealings God often meets with a man and speaks particularly to the evil of his heart, as he did with Joseph’s brothers over their selling him into Egypt. This makes the man reflect on his sin and judge himself for it in particular. God makes it the voice of the danger, affliction, trouble, or sickness he is in or under. Sometimes, in reading the word, God makes a man stop at something that cuts him to the heart and shakes him about his present condition. More often God meets with men in the hearing of the word preached, his great ordinance for conviction, conversion, and edification. In that ordinance God often hews men with the sword of his word. He strikes directly at their dearly loved lust, startles the sinner, and makes him pledge himself to mortify and give up the evil of his heart. Now, suppose his lust has taken such hold on him that it forces him to break these bonds of the Lord and throw off these cords. Suppose it overcomes these convictions and gets back into its old position, and can heal the wounds it received this way. That soul is in a sad condition.

The evils that come with such a frame of heart are beyond words. Every particular warning to a man in such a state is a priceless mercy. How then he despises God in them, when he holds out against them! And what infinite patience this is in God, that he does not cast off such a man and swear in his wrath that he will never enter into his rest!

These and many other evidences there are of a lust that is dangerous, if not deadly. As our Savior said of the evil spirit, “This kind cannot come out, except by prayer and fasting,” so I say of lusts of this kind. An ordinary course of mortification will not do it; extraordinary ways must be settled on.

This is the first particular direction: consider whether the lust or sin you are fighting with has any of these dangerous symptoms accompanying it.

Before I go on, I must give you one caution by the way, so that no one is deceived by what has been said. I say that the things and evils mentioned above may befall true believers. But let no man who finds the same things in himself conclude from them that he is a true believer. These are the evils believers may fall into and be ensnared by, not the things that make a man a believer. A man might as well conclude that he is a believer because he is an adulterer, since David, a true believer, fell into adultery, as conclude it from the signs above, which are the evils of sin and Satan in the hearts of believers. The seventh chapter of Romans contains the description of a regenerate man. Suppose a man considers what is said there of his dark side, of his unregenerate part, of the indwelling power and violence of sin remaining in him, and because he finds the like in himself, concludes that he is a regenerate man. He will be mistaken in his reckoning. It is just as if you were to argue: a wise man may be sick and wounded, and may even do some things foolishly; therefore everyone who is sick and wounded and does things foolishly is a wise man. Or it is as if a poor, deformed creature heard someone speak of a beautiful person, and say that he had a mark or a scar that greatly disfigured him, and then concluded that because he himself has scars and moles and warts, he too is beautiful. If you want evidence that you are a believer, it must come from the things that make men believers. The man who has these evils in himself may safely conclude, “If I am a believer, I am a most miserable one.” But to know that any man is a believer, he must look for other evidence, if he wants peace.
